¶
'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To move, throw, put, pull, carry, or the like, with a sudden, quick motion; snatch: usually followed by some preposition or adverb, as ''away'', ''from'', ''in'', ''into'', ''off'', ''on'', ''out'', ''up'', etc.: as, to ''whip out'' a sword or a revolver.

{{smaller block|I ''whipt'' me ''behind'' the arras. {{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, i. 3. 63.}}
<poem>
In came Clause,
The old lame beggar, and ''whipt up'' Master Goswin
Under his arm, away with him.
</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Beggars’ Bush, v. 1.}}
She then ''whipped off'' her domino, and threw it over Mrs. Atkinson. {{right|''Fielding'', Amelia, x. 3.}}}}
